WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.042 --> 00:00:03.253
So we get to ride in an 
Uber with the Uber CEO.

00:00:03.253 --> 00:00:04.546
Yeah, that'll be pretty cool.

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:06.089
Do you get special 
treatment on the app?

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:08.299
I do not get special 
treatment on the app.

00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:08.967
I wish I did.

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:10.093
If we call an electric 
car right now,

00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:11.886
whose comes first, 
yours or mine?

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:13.513
It's an open marketplace.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:14.222
It depends.

00:00:14.222 --> 00:00:15.598
You never know 
who comes in first.

00:00:15.598 --> 00:00:16.474
Alright, let's do it.

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:17.976
Let’s pick our destination.

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:19.394
You can see lots of options.

00:00:19.394 --> 00:00:21.813
You can go Comfort Electric. 
You can go Green.

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:23.648
There's like 10 options 
here, right now.

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:24.524
More than 10 options.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:25.275
A lot of options.

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.028
Are you at all worried about 
overwhelming consumers?

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:31.865
You know, going green is 
going to take some time.

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:34.409
And we want to give as 
many choices to consumers

00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:36.453
so that they can pick to go green

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:38.288
on their terms, the 
way that they want.

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:39.122
But let's go Comfort Electric

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:40.623
since there are 
two of us in the car.

00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:41.624
Let’s do it.

00:00:48.631 --> 00:00:49.591
Here's Hailey.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:50.717
Hello.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:51.426
Hi. How are you doing?

00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:52.677
How are you? 
I'm good.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:57.057
What is Uber's current 
carbon footprint?

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.600
It's pretty significant.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.435
Generally, the footprint is based

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:02.395
on essentially all 
the miles driven.

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:05.440
We completed 2 billion 
trips this last quarter.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.400
And we've got to do 
everything that we can

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.693
in order to reduce that footprint.

00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:11.821
And we’ve committed 
that by 2030 in the US,

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:14.491
and Europe, and Canada,
we're going to be all-electric.

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:17.994
And by 2040, we're going to
be electric all over the world.

00:01:17.994 --> 00:01:19.412
That's an ambitious target.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.790
Uber wants to cut 
emissions entirely.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.001
So for this ride-hailing giant,
that means getting to total

00:01:25.001 --> 00:01:28.797
electrification, which would be 
nothing short of a green miracle.

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:31.382
It's all part of the company's
sustainability agenda

00:01:31.382 --> 00:01:34.511
called Go-Get Zero.

00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:38.098
The goal is to go electric
and create the incentives

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:41.351
for drivers to go electric 
as quickly as possible

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:42.894
and riders to pick electric as well.

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:45.480
So the smart charging 
technology is allowing

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.941
 drivers to know when
and where to charge,

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.944
to feel safe to get
routes that are efficient.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.153
Then on the rider side,

00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:56.074
is actually a feature for you 
to see how much you're saving

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:57.492
 in terms of emissions.

00:01:57.492 --> 00:01:59.410
So when you get that Green car

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.204
or when you get 
that Comfort Electric,

00:02:01.204 --> 00:02:02.997
we’ll show you how much

00:02:02.997 --> 00:02:04.374
you're helping out 
the environment,

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:06.668
how many emissions 
you're saving.

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:08.962
We have to get 
that flywheel together.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:10.755
We need drivers 
to choose electric.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:11.965
And then we're going to put more

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.175
and more electric options
in front of consumers as well.

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:16.636
How much more does 
it cost to ride Electric?

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:18.471
If you're taking Green,

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.975
actually, it's the same 
exact price as an UberX.

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:23.268
So are you taking the hit on that?

00:02:23.268 --> 00:02:24.769
Or does the driver 
take the hit on that?

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:25.770
Where does that cost?

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:26.521
We take the hit.

00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:30.150
So we've said that we are 
investing $800 million

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.986
in the transition to electric.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.029
And we've got to make it
economically sensible

00:02:35.029 --> 00:02:36.030
for drivers.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:40.410
So for example, our take rate 
on Electric rides is lower.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.370
Because right now, generally,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.622
EVs cost more than
combustion engines.

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:54.299
So I asked my Uber driver today

00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:58.094
if he would switch to electric
and he said absolutely not.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:02:59.637
He said the grid's not ready.

00:02:59.637 --> 00:03:00.471
Mhmm.

00:03:00.471 --> 00:03:02.265
What about the range anxiety?

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:04.100
What about the time
it takes to charge

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.935
when you could be driving?

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:06.686
You could be making money.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.062
Yeah, those are real issues.

00:03:08.062 --> 00:03:11.191
And I do think that as 
it relates to charging,

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:15.028
we're introducing now smart 
charge and routing technology.

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:15.612
How does that work?

00:03:15.612 --> 00:03:19.240
What it does is when you 
go out and start driving

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.201
based on your earnings
opportunity in the city, 

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:23.203
we will identify

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:25.205
smart locations
for you to charge.

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:26.331
We’ll tell you when 
you should charge.

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:29.542
Because even the charging
costs go up and down

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:32.003
 on a daily basis or based
on different locations.

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:34.047
And another really cool 
technology that we're

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:35.632
 introducing is actually 
smart routing as well.

00:03:35.632 --> 00:03:38.718
We use AI algorithms so that if,

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:42.722
let's say you're at a 25% 
charge or a 30% charge,

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:46.434
we won't send you 
on a really long trip.

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:47.977
But we're not going to 
send you on a trip

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:49.479
that is in a charging desert.

00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:50.355
We're going to make sure that

00:03:50.355 --> 00:03:51.981
there are plenty 
of chargers around.

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:01.366
Time to charge up.

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:03.534
Alright, yes it is.

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:05.161
Does the charge time bother you?

00:04:06.162 --> 00:04:07.664
Honestly, it does.

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:08.915
Yeah.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:09.958
It's different.

00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:11.334
It's something to get used to.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:13.044
I don't like waiting too long

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:14.087
before I have to go.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.213
Why did you buy a Tesla?

00:04:15.213 --> 00:04:15.922
The pricing.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.840
Like with gas right 
now, it’s so expensive.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.134
I come from spending 
$400 a week

00:04:20.134 --> 00:04:21.219
to spending $150 a week.

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:22.303
And that's a major deal,

00:04:22.303 --> 00:04:24.097
especially when it 
comes to budgeting.

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:25.848
Do you think Uber’s 
treating drivers right?

00:04:25.848 --> 00:04:27.934
I think they should be 
able to pay a little more.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.852
I think the benefits 
should be better.

00:04:29.852 --> 00:04:30.395
Like what?

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:33.648
Like health care, paid time off,

00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:35.316
just regular benefits that,

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:37.235
you know, every other job gets.

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:47.370
The biggest obstacle is getting

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.706
all of these independent parties,

00:04:49.706 --> 00:04:50.999
all of these companies,

00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:53.793
all of these governments
and then consumers

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:56.254
 to work together

00:04:56.254 --> 00:05:00.383
in a way for a single goal,
which is electrification.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:01.884
So we're saying, hey, let's as Uber,

00:05:01.884 --> 00:05:04.137
we should take a lead
in electrification.

00:05:04.137 --> 00:05:05.680
And in the end, it's good 
business to go green.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:06.264
 Right.

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:10.435
San Francisco still hasn't 
recovered from the pandemic.

00:05:10.435 --> 00:05:13.104
What are Uber levels?

00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:14.647
Well, when we look at 
San Francisco in terms of

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:17.734
Uber and how it's come
back pre-pandemic,

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:21.112
San Francisco honestly 
is pretty low.

00:05:21.112 --> 00:05:23.114
On a global basis 
in terms of trips,

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:25.700
we're well above 
pre-pandemic level.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.619
We're going through
a particularly difficult time

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:31.956
but never bet against 
the tech sector

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:33.791
and never bet against 
San Francisco coming back.

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:35.460
Well I hope you're right,
because it's my home too.

